# json — JSON, in pure Luce.
#
# Parse text into a value, ask a value what it is with `match`, build
# values directly, write one back out.  Nothing here is a builtin,
# nothing here imports anything, and nothing here touches the host:
# `parse` takes a str and answers a `Json`, and where the str
# came from is the caller's business.
#
# **A JSON value is a union, because that is what a JSON value is.**
# RFC 8259 says a value is one of six things and two of the six
# contain more values; `union Json` says the same sentence in Luce, and
# `match` is the only way to read it — so asking a number for its
# members is not a checked error, it is a program that does not
# compile.  This module is the customer `docs/UNION.md` was argued
# from, and it is now written on top of it.
#
#     match doc:
#         object(fields):
#             let port = fields.get("port")
#             ...
#
# **Eager, and the value is the answer.**  A parse turns `"1e3"` into
# 1000.0 and `"é"` into é once, on the way in, and what a caller
# holds afterwards is the number and the text rather than a span
# pointing back at the document.  The document's bytes are not kept.
# That is the trade this module makes and it is worth naming: the
# previous design was lazy — every value remembered the text it was
# written as, and `write` put those exact tokens back — because
# without unions a value had to be an index into a flat tape.  With a
# union the value *is* the value, so `write` re-encodes rather than
# echoes, and the property it keeps is the one that matters:
# **parsing what `write` produced gives an equal value, and writing
# that gives identical text.**
#
# **Memory is automatic.**  A `Json` carries objects — `list[Json]` and
# `map[str, Json]` are reference-counted containers — and they are
# freed when the last reference to them goes away.  There is no arena,
# no collector and no `deinit` to remember.
#
# **Building one reads the way taking one apart does.**  The map and
# the list *are* the builder:
#
#     var fields = new map[str, Json]
#     fields["name"] = Json.text(value = "luce")
#     fields["tags"] = Json.array(items = [Json.integer(value = 1)])
#     let doc = Json.object(fields = fields)
#
# **Written against the normative documents.**  The grammar is RFC
# 8259 — §2 the structural characters and the four whitespace bytes,
# §3 the three literal names, §4 objects, §5 arrays, §6 numbers, §7
# strings and their escapes, §8.1 encoding, §8.2 unpaired surrogates,
# §9 nesting limits.  ECMA-404 says the same grammar in other words
# and is cited where the two documents leave a reader a choice.  Every
# rule below names the clause it implements, and the specification
# (`src/luce/specs/json_spec.zig`) proves each one on both engines.
#
# **Reading a file** is three calls, and the module deliberately makes
# none of them:
#
#     let data = try files.read_bytes(path)
#     let text = strings.from_bytes(data) else ""
#     let doc = try json.parse(text)
#
# **UTF-8 needs no checking here** (RFC 8259 §8.1).  The input is a
# Luce str, which is valid UTF-8 by construction, so the encoding
# question every other parser has to answer was answered before the
# text arrived.  What is left is the grammar, and that is all this
# module checks.
#
# **The calls this module makes that other parsers argue about**, each
# with its reason:
#
# * **A number is read by the notation it was written in.**  JSON has
#   one number type and Luce has two, and a language with no implicit
#   narrowing cannot hand a `i64` out of a `f64` without inventing
#   or discarding information.  So `42` is `Json.integer` and `4.2`,
#   `42.0` and `4.2e1` are all `Json.real`; `as_i64` exposes that
#   distinction without rounding or re-reading source text. Zig's `std.json`, serde_json,
#   Jackson and System.Text.Json all split the same way and for the
#   same reason.  A whole number too large for a `i64` is a `real`,
#   which is where its precision honestly is.
# * **A number past what a `f64` can hold is refused**, by name, at
#   the u8 (RFC 8259 §6: *"This specification allows implementations
#   to set limits on the range and precision of numbers accepted"*).
#   The alternative, now that values are eager, would be storing an
#   infinity — and infinity is not JSON, so it could never be written
#   back.  `1e309` is an error with a sentence; `1e308` is a number.
# * **Nesting is bounded at 64** (RFC 8259 §9 explicitly allows a
#   parser to set a limit).  A tree is walked by recursion — this
#   module's own parser and writer recurse once per level, and so will
#   every caller that reads one — and loom lets a Luce program nest
#   128 calls before it traps `call_depth_exceeded`
#   (`src/apps/host.zig`). Sixty-four is half of that: a document
#   this module accepts can be parsed, walked and written from a call
#   stack that is already sixty deep.  The old bound was 128, chosen
#   when the parser was iterative and only the *caller* recursed;
#   a union has no iterative walk to offer, so the number moved to
#   stay true.
# * **A duplicate member name resolves to the last one written**
#   (RFC 8259 §4: names SHOULD be unique, and the behaviour when they
#   are not is unpredictable).  An object is a `map[str, Json]`, so
#   the second `"a"` replaces the first in place — the same thing
#   JavaScript's `JSON.parse`, Python's `json` and Go's
#   `encoding/json` all do, and unlike the previous design the
#   document does *not* keep both: a mapping with two entries under
#   one key is not a mapping.  Order is insertion order, so a member's
#   position is where it was first named.
# * **An unpaired surrogate escape is refused** (RFC 8259 §8.2 warns
#   about them; ECMA-404 permits the code unit sequence).  A Luce
#   str is UTF-8 and there is no UTF-8 for half a surrogate pair,
#   so the honest answers are "refuse" or "quietly substitute U+FFFD",
#   and this module refuses by name at the u8 where it happened.
#   A well-formed pair — `𝄞` — is one codepoint, 𝄞, and is
#   read as one.
# * **NaN and Infinity are not JSON** (RFC 8259 §6 lists exactly one
#   number grammar and no names in it), so they are refused like any
#   other unknown word on the way in — and a `Json.real` built by hand
#   out of one traps on the way out, because there is no text this
#   module could write that would read back as what it was given.
#
# Errors name a Unicode-scalar position, because that is the position
# the parser genuinely has and a line and column would be a second thing to keep
# right.  `parse` answers `Json!` and not `Json?` because there are
# many distinct ways for a document to be wrong and the one it met is
# worth carrying (docs/FAILURE.md).

# --- the vocabulary of the grammar -----------------------------------
#
# RFC 8259 §2: the six structural characters, the two quoting marks,
# and nothing else in this module is written as a bare number.

private const quote_mark: char = '"'
private const backslash: char = '\\'
private const brace_open: char = '{'
private const brace_close: char = '}'
private const bracket_open: char = '['
private const bracket_close: char = ']'
private const colon: char = ':'
private const comma: char = ','
private const minus: char = '-'
private const plus: char = '+'
private const point: char = '.'
private const digit_zero: char = '0'
private const digit_nine: char = '9'
private const letter_e: char = 'e'
private const letter_e_capital: char = 'E'
private const letter_u: char = 'u'

# RFC 8259 §9 leaves the limit to the parser. This one is half of
# loom's call-depth policy, because a tree is walked by recursion and
# both ends of that walk — this module and its caller — have to fit
# inside the one budget.
private const max_depth = 64

# --- what a value is -------------------------------------------------

# One JSON value (RFC 8259 §3 the literal names, §4 objects, §5
# arrays, §6 numbers, §7 strings).
#
# Seven members for six kinds: JSON's one number type arrives here as
# the two Luce has, and which one a value holds is the notation it was
# written in.  `null` is first, so it is also the zero — what a
# `var j: Json` starts at and what fills a `new array[Json](n)`.
#
# The names are the specification's own except for the str kind:
# `str` is a reserved name in Luce (it is the type, and the
# conversion constructor named for it), so JSON's str is spelled
# `text` here — the same word `strings.from_bytes` uses for the same
# thing.
union Json:
    null
    boolean(value: bool)
    integer(value: i64)
    real(value: f64)
    text(value: str)
    array(items: list[Json])
    object(fields: map[str, Json])

    # True for JSON's `null` and nothing else — which is a different
    # question from "is this value missing", and that is the point of
    # having both (`member` answers absence).
    func is_null() -> bool:
        match self:
            null:
                return true
            else:
                return false

    # `true` or `false`; absent for every other member.
    func as_bool() -> bool?:
        match self:
            boolean(value):
                return value
            else:
                return none

    # The number as a whole number — absent unless it was written as
    # one.  `42` answers 42; `4.2`, `42.0` and `4.2e1` all answer
    # `none` and are read with `as_f64`.  A number written as a
    # real is a real, and answering absence says so, where truncating
    # would throw information away silently and rounding would invent
    # it.
    func as_i64() -> i64?:
        match self:
            integer(value):
                return value
            else:
                return none

    # The number as f64, whichever numeric member it uses. Integer
    # payloads are converted explicitly here.
    func as_f64() -> f64?:
        match self:
            integer(value):
                return f64(value)
            real(value):
                return value
            else:
                return none

    # The str, decoded — the escapes were spent on the way in, so
    # this is the text the document meant.  Absent for every other
    # member.
    func as_text() -> str?:
        match self:
            text(value):
                return value
            else:
                return none

    # How many members an object has, or elements an array has.  A
    # leaf answers 0.
    func count() -> i64:
        match self:
            array(items):
                return len(items)
            object(fields):
                return len(fields)
            else:
                return 0

    # The member of an object named `name`, or absent — for a name
    # that is not there and for a value that is not an object, which
    # are the same news to a caller who was looking for a field.
    #
    # **The answer is a value**, read out of the container: a leaf that
    # is a number or a str, or a subtree whose inner containers are
    # references shared with the document.  A walk that only means to
    # *read* should say `match` and reach the map itself directly.
    func member(name: str) -> Json?:
        match self:
            object(fields):
                if fields.has(name):
                    return fields[name]
                return none
            else:
                return none

    # The element at `index` of an array, or the member at that
    # position of an object.  Out of range traps: `count()` is right
    # there, and asking past it is a bug rather than news
    # (docs/FAILURE.md) — which is the same split the language's own
    # containers make, where `xs[i]` traps and `m.get(k)` does not.
    #
    # A copy, for `member`'s reason — and on an object it also walks
    # the names to find the position, so a loop that wants every
    # member should `match` and iterate the map once instead.
    func element(index: i64) -> Json:
        match self:
            array(items):
                if index < 0 or index >= len(items):
                    trap(f"json: no element {index} in a value of {len(items)}")
                return items[index]
            object(fields):
                let names = fields.keys()
                if index < 0 or index >= len(names):
                    trap(f"json: no element {index} in a value of {len(names)}")
                return fields[names[index]]
            else:
                trap(f"json: no element {index} in a value of 0")

    # The value as JSON text, with nothing between the tokens.
    #
    # Not an echo of whatever text this value was parsed from — the
    # value is what survived that parse — but a re-encoding of the
    # value itself, so `parse(j.write())` is a value equal to `j` and
    # writing *that* gives the same bytes again.
    func write() -> str:
        let printer = Printer(out = new builder, spaces = 0)
        printer.put(self, 0)
        return printer.out.build()

    # The same, indented: every member and element on its own line,
    # `spaces` further in than its container.  `spaces` of 0 is
    # `write`.
    func pretty(spaces: i64) -> str:
        if spaces < 0:
            trap("json: pretty needs spaces >= 0")
        let printer = Printer(out = new builder, spaces = spaces)
        printer.put(self, 0)
        return printer.out.build()

# --- parsing ---------------------------------------------------------

# Read one JSON document (RFC 8259 §2: exactly one value, with
# whitespace allowed around it).
#
# Everything that can be wrong with the text is an error naming the
# scalar position where it happened.  The document's boundaries are
# checked here — a text with no value in it, and a text with more
# after its one value — and everything between them is the Parser's.
func parse(content: str) -> Json!:
    var reader = Parser(content = content, at = 0)
    reader.skip_blank()
    if reader.at >= len(content):
        error("json: there is no value in the text")
    let value = try reader.read_value(0)
    reader.skip_blank()
    if reader.at != len(content):
        error(f"json: text after the document's one value, at position {reader.at}")
    return value

# The read head over the document: the text, and the scalar the parser
# stands on.  Every reading method consumes what it read by advancing
# `at`, so a value and the position after it never travel as a pair —
# the cursor *is* the second half of every answer, and the position a
# diagnostic names is the one the parser genuinely holds.
private struct Parser:
    private content: str
    private at: i64

    # One value, with the read head left after it.
    #
    # Recursive descent: the shape of the grammar is the shape of the
    # walk, and a union has no flat tape to walk instead.
    #
    # **The two container grammars are inlined here rather than written
    # as `read_array` and `read_object`, and the reason is
    # `max_depth`.**  A nested value would otherwise cost *two* frames
    # a level — the reader for the container and the reader for the
    # value inside it — and the budget this module has to divide is
    # loom's 128 nested calls, shared with whatever the caller does
    # with the answer.  One frame a level is what lets the bound be a
    # number worth having.  This is the one place in the module where a
    # function is longer than its parts because the parts would cost
    # something real (docs/CODING_GUIDE.md).
    private func read_value(depth: i64) -> Json!:
        self.skip_blank()
        if self.at >= len(self.content):
            error(f"json: a value was expected at position {self.at}")
        let head = self.content[self.at]

        # RFC 8259 §5: values separated by commas, between brackets.
        if head == bracket_open:
            if depth >= max_depth:
                error(f"json: values nested deeper than {max_depth}, at position {self.at}")
            let opened = self.at
            var items = new list[Json]
            self.at += 1
            self.skip_blank()
            # The loop leaves with the head on the closing bracket,
            # whichever way it got there, so the value is built once at
            # the bottom, from the list assembled above.
            if self.at >= len(self.content) or self.content[self.at] != bracket_close:
                var listed = false
                while not listed:
                    let element = try self.read_value(depth + 1)
                    items.append(element)
                    self.skip_blank()
                    if self.at >= len(self.content):
                        error(f"json: the text ends inside the array opened at position {opened}")
                    let mark = self.content[self.at]
                    if mark == comma:
                        self.at += 1
                    elif mark == bracket_close:
                        listed = true
                    else:
                        error(f"json: a comma or a close was expected at position {self.at}")
            self.at += 1
            return Json.array(items = items)

        # RFC 8259 §4: names, colons, values, commas, between braces.
        if head == brace_open:
            if depth >= max_depth:
                error(f"json: values nested deeper than {max_depth}, at position {self.at}")
            let opened = self.at
            var fields = new map[str, Json]
            self.at += 1
            self.skip_blank()
            if self.at >= len(self.content) or self.content[self.at] != brace_close:
                var mapped = false
                while not mapped:
                    self.skip_blank()
                    if self.at >= len(self.content) or self.content[self.at] != quote_mark:
                        error(f"json: a member name must be a string, at position {self.at}")
                    let name = try self.read_string()
                    self.skip_blank()
                    if self.at >= len(self.content) or self.content[self.at] != colon:
                        error(f"json: a member name must be followed by a colon, at position {self.at}")
                    self.at += 1
                    let member = try self.read_value(depth + 1)
                    # §4 leaves a repeated name to the parser; a map is
                    # a mapping, so the last one written is what the
                    # name means, in the place the first one claimed.
                    fields[name] = member
                    self.skip_blank()
                    if self.at >= len(self.content):
                        error(f"json: the text ends inside the object opened at position {opened}")
                    let mark = self.content[self.at]
                    if mark == comma:
                        self.at += 1
                    elif mark == brace_close:
                        mapped = true
                    else:
                        error(f"json: a comma or a close was expected at position {self.at}")
            self.at += 1
            return Json.object(fields = fields)

        if head == quote_mark:
            let spelled = try self.read_string()
            return Json.text(value = spelled)
        if head == minus or is_digit(head):
            return try self.read_number()
        if self.word("true"):
            return Json.boolean(value = true)
        if self.word("false"):
            return Json.boolean(value = false)
        if self.word("null"):
            return Json.null
        error(f"json: a value was expected at position {self.at}")

    # RFC 8259 §2: whitespace is exactly these four bytes — space,
    # horizontal tab, line feed and carriage return.  Nothing else is
    # whitespace, which is why a vertical tab or a form feed between
    # two tokens is an error rather than a shrug.
    private func skip_blank():
        while self.at < len(self.content):
            let c = self.content[self.at]
            if c != ' ' and c != '\t' and c != '\n' and c != char(13):
                return
            self.at += 1

    # True when `spelling` stands at the read head, which then moves
    # past it.  A miss moves nothing, so the refusal after the last
    # miss still names the position a value was expected at.
    private func word(spelling: str) -> bool:
        if self.at + len(spelling) > len(self.content):
            return false
        for i in range(0, len(spelling)):
            if self.content[self.at + i] != spelling[i]:
                return false
        self.at += len(spelling)
        return true

    # RFC 8259 §7.  The read head stands on the opening quote and is
    # left after the closing one; the answer is the text the token
    # spells.
    #
    # Almost every str in almost every document holds no escape at all,
    # and that one is a slice of the input and needs no builder.  The
    # first backslash hands the rest of the token to `read_escaped`,
    # which does.  The two loops repeat two of §7's sentences — a
    # control character must be escaped, and a str must be closed —
    # because they are asking different questions of the same scalars;
    # folding them together would mean building a str for every token
    # that did not need one.
    private func read_string() -> str!:
        let opened = self.at
        self.at += 1
        while self.at < len(self.content):
            let c = self.content[self.at]
            if c == quote_mark:
                let spelled = self.content[opened + 1:self.at]
                self.at += 1
                return spelled
            elif c == backslash:
                return try self.read_escaped(opened)
            elif u32(c) < 32:
                # §7: the control characters must be escaped.  A raw
                # newline inside a str is the usual way to meet this.
                error(f"json: an unescaped control character inside a string, at position {self.at}")
            self.at += 1
        error(f"json: the string opened at position {opened} is never closed")

    # The rest of a str token, decoded, with the read head on its
    # first backslash.  `opened` is the opening quote, kept for the
    # sentence a str that never closes deserves.
    private func read_escaped(opened: i64) -> str!:
        var out = new builder
        out.append(self.content[opened + 1:self.at])
        while self.at < len(self.content):
            let c = self.content[self.at]
            if c == quote_mark:
                self.at += 1
                return out.build()
            elif c == backslash:
                try self.decode_escape(out)
            elif u32(c) < 32:
                error(f"json: an unescaped control character inside a string, at position {self.at}")
            else:
                # A run of ordinary scalars goes in whole: it can hold
                # whole characters of any width, and it ends at a
                # backslash or at the closing quote, both of which are
                # ASCII.
                let run = self.at
                while self.at < len(self.content) and self.content[self.at] != backslash and self.content[self.at] != quote_mark and u32(self.content[self.at]) >= 32:
                    self.at += 1
                out.append(self.content[run:self.at])
        error(f"json: the string opened at position {opened} is never closed")

    # RFC 8259 §7's eight escapes and `\uXXXX`.  Puts what the escape
    # spells into `out`; the read head stands on the backslash and is
    # left after the whole escape.  The diagnostics name the backslash,
    # so its position is kept even as the head moves past it.
    private func decode_escape(out: builder) -> !:
        let slash = self.at
        if slash + 1 >= len(self.content):
            error(f"json: the text ends inside an escape, at position {slash}")
        let c = self.content[slash + 1]
        if c == letter_u:
            # A high surrogate is half of a codepoint and must be
            # followed by its low half (§7); a low one on its own is
            # not a codepoint at all (§8.2).  Refusing both is what
            # keeps every str this module accepts decodable into UTF-8,
            # which is the only thing a Luce str can be.
            self.at = slash + 2
            var code = try self.read_hex4()
            if code >= 55296 and code <= 56319:
                # The head stands after the four digits, where the low
                # half's own `\u` must be.
                if self.at + 1 >= len(self.content) or self.content[self.at] != backslash or self.content[self.at + 1] != letter_u:
                    error(f"json: a high surrogate escape at position {slash} is not followed by its low half")
                self.at += 2
                let low = try self.read_hex4()
                if low < 56320 or low > 57343:
                    error(f"json: a high surrogate escape at position {slash} is followed by something that is not a low surrogate")
                # §7's own example is this exact pair: 𝄞 is one
                # codepoint written as two escapes.
                code = 65536 + (code - 55296) * 1024 + (low - 56320)
            elif code >= 56320 and code <= 57343:
                error(f"json: a low surrogate escape stands alone at position {slash}")
            out.append(str(char(code)))
            return
        if c == quote_mark or c == backslash or c == '/':
            out.append_ascii(i64(u32(c)))
        elif c == 'b':
            out.append_ascii(8)
        elif c == 'f':
            out.append_ascii(12)
        elif c == 'n':
            out.append_ascii(10)
        elif c == 'r':
            out.append_ascii(13)
        elif c == 't':
            out.append_ascii(9)
        else:
            error(f"json: unknown escape at position {slash}")
        self.at = slash + 2

    # Four hexadecimal digits at the read head, as the number they
    # spell.
    private func read_hex4() -> i64!:
        let start = self.at
        if start + 4 > len(self.content):
            error(f"json: an escape at position {start} wants four hexadecimal digits and the text ends first")
        var value: i64 = 0
        for i in range(0, 4):
            let c = self.content[start + i]
            var part = -1
            if c >= digit_zero and c <= digit_nine:
                part = i64(u32(c)) - i64(u32(digit_zero))
            elif c >= 'a' and c <= 'f':
                part = i64(u32(c)) - i64(u32('a')) + 10
            elif c >= 'A' and c <= 'F':
                part = i64(u32(c)) - i64(u32('A')) + 10
            else:
                error(f"json: an escape at position {start} wants four hexadecimal digits")
            value = value * 16 + part
        self.at = start + 4
        return value

    # RFC 8259 §6, and the whole of it: an optional minus, an integer
    # part that is either a single zero or a digit str not starting
    # with one, an optional fraction with at least one digit, and an
    # optional exponent with at least one digit.  `.5`, `5.`, `01`,
    # `+1`, `1e`, `0x10`, `NaN` and `Infinity` are all outside it, and
    # none of them reaches a caller as a number.
    #
    # The notation decides the member: written whole, it is an
    # `integer`; written with a fraction or an exponent, it is a
    # `real`.  A whole number too large for a `i64` is a `real` too,
    # because that is where its precision honestly is — and a number
    # too large for a `f64` is refused, since there is nothing left to
    # hold it with.
    private func read_number() -> Json!:
        let start = self.at
        if self.content[self.at] == minus:
            self.at += 1
        if self.at >= len(self.content) or not is_digit(self.content[self.at]):
            error(f"json: a number needs a digit, at position {self.at}")
        if self.content[self.at] == digit_zero:
            self.at += 1
            if self.at < len(self.content) and is_digit(self.content[self.at]):
                error(f"json: a number may not have a leading zero, at position {start}")
        else:
            while self.at < len(self.content) and is_digit(self.content[self.at]):
                self.at += 1
        var whole = true
        if self.at < len(self.content) and self.content[self.at] == point:
            whole = false
            self.at += 1
            if self.at >= len(self.content) or not is_digit(self.content[self.at]):
                error(f"json: a fraction needs a digit after the point, at position {self.at}")
            while self.at < len(self.content) and is_digit(self.content[self.at]):
                self.at += 1
        if self.at < len(self.content) and (self.content[self.at] == letter_e or self.content[self.at] == letter_e_capital):
            whole = false
            self.at += 1
            if self.at < len(self.content) and (self.content[self.at] == plus or self.content[self.at] == minus):
                self.at += 1
            if self.at >= len(self.content) or not is_digit(self.content[self.at]):
                error(f"json: an exponent needs a digit, at position {self.at}")
            while self.at < len(self.content) and is_digit(self.content[self.at]):
                self.at += 1
        let token = self.content[start:self.at]
        if whole:
            let counted = parse_i64(token)
            if counted != none:
                return Json.integer(value = counted)
        let measured = parse_f64(token)
        if measured != none:
            return Json.real(value = measured)
        error(f"json: the number at position {start} is past what an f64 can hold")

private func is_digit(c: char) -> bool:
    return c >= digit_zero and c <= digit_nine

# --- writing ---------------------------------------------------------

# Text as a JSON str, quotes and escapes and all (RFC 8259 §7).
#
# The two escapes that are required — the quote and the backslash —
# and the control characters, which the six short names cover where
# they can and `\u00xx` covers where they cannot.  Everything else
# goes in as itself: the solidus needs no escape (§7 permits one and
# does not ask for it), and a character outside ASCII is UTF-8 in the
# output exactly as it was in the input, which is what §8.1 recommends
# and what makes a `\u` escape unnecessary here.
#
# This is the door for a program that builds JSON text of its own out
# of a builder rather than a value, and it is the same escaping
# `write` puts around a str and a member name.
func quote(content: str) -> str:
    let printer = Printer(out = new builder, spaces = 0)
    printer.put_quoted(content)
    return printer.out.build()

# The write head: the builder every level appends into, and the indent
# the caller asked for — `spaces` of 0 is the compact form.  Both are
# settled at construction, and the builder is a reference, so nothing
# here writes a field: a Printer sits happily in a `let`.
private struct Printer:
    private out: builder
    private spaces: i64

    # One value and everything under it, as JSON text.
    #
    # Recursive, for the reason the reader is: the tree is the shape of
    # the walk.  Every member of the union is written here and there is
    # no `else`, so the day a member is added this function stops
    # compiling and names it.
    private func put(held: Json, depth: i64):
        match held:
            null:
                self.out.append("null")
            boolean(value):
                self.out.append(str(value))
            integer(value):
                self.out.append(str(value))
            real(value):
                self.put_real(value)
            text(value):
                self.put_quoted(value)
            array(items):
                if len(items) == 0:
                    self.out.append("[]")
                else:
                    self.out.append("[")
                    var first = true
                    for item in items:
                        if not first:
                            self.out.append(",")
                        self.put_break(depth + 1)
                        self.put(item, depth + 1)
                        first = false
                    self.put_break(depth)
                    self.out.append("]")
            object(fields):
                if len(fields) == 0:
                    self.out.append("{}")
                else:
                    self.out.append("{")
                    var first = true
                    for name, entry in fields:
                        if not first:
                            self.out.append(",")
                        self.put_break(depth + 1)
                        self.put_quoted(name)
                        self.out.append(":")
                        if self.spaces > 0:
                            self.out.append(" ")
                        self.put(entry, depth + 1)
                        first = false
                    self.put_break(depth)
                    self.out.append("}")

    # A str's text between its quotes, escaped as §7 asks — the one
    # quoting path, shared by a value's text, a member's name, and the
    # free `quote`.
    private func put_quoted(content: str):
        self.out.append_ascii(i64(u32(quote_mark)))
        var run = 0
        var at = 0
        while at < len(content):
            let c = content[at]
            if c == quote_mark or c == backslash or u32(c) < 32:
                if at > run:
                    self.out.append(content[run:at])
                self.put_escape(c)
                run = at + 1
            at += 1
        self.out.append(content[run:])
        self.out.append_ascii(i64(u32(quote_mark)))

    private func put_escape(c: char):
        self.out.append_ascii(i64(u32(backslash)))
        if c == quote_mark or c == backslash:
            self.out.append_ascii(i64(u32(c)))
        elif c == char(8):
            self.out.append_ascii(98)
        elif c == char(12):
            self.out.append_ascii(102)
        elif c == '\n':
            self.out.append_ascii(110)
        elif c == char(13):
            self.out.append_ascii(114)
        elif c == '\t':
            self.out.append_ascii(116)
        else:
            # Every other control character, four hexadecimal digits of
            # it.  Lowercase, as JavaScript and Python both write them.
            self.out.append_ascii(i64(u32(letter_u)))
            self.out.append_ascii(i64(u32(digit_zero)))
            self.out.append_ascii(i64(u32(digit_zero)))
            let code = i64(u32(c))
            self.out.append_ascii(hex_digit(code >> 4 & 15))
            self.out.append_ascii(hex_digit(code & 15))

    # A real, written so that reading it back gives a real.
    #
    # `str(d)` answers the shortest decimal that reads back as the same
    # f64, which is what a JSON writer wants — but a whole-valued one
    # comes out with no fraction, and `42` read back is an `integer`.
    # So the point is put on, the way Python's `json` does, and the
    # member survives the round trip.
    #
    # What `str(d)` does *not* do is write an exponent, so `1e300`
    # leaves here as its three hundred and one digits.  That is longer
    # than a JSON writer would choose and it is still the same number:
    # the text reads back as the identical f64, which is the property
    # this module owes.  Shortening it is a job for `str(f64)`, where
    # every other caller would get it too, and not for a private helper
    # here.
    private func put_real(value: f64):
        let written = str(value)
        if written == "inf" or written == "-inf" or written == "nan":
            # "inf", "-inf", "nan": RFC 8259 §6 has no grammar for any
            # of them, so there is no text to write.  Nothing this
            # module parses can get here; a value built by hand out of
            # an overflowing sum can.
            trap(f"json: {written} is not a number JSON can write")
        self.out.append(written)
        if written.find_byte(u8(46), 0) < 0:
            self.out.append(".0")

    # A line break and one level of indentation, or nothing at all when
    # the printer was built for no spaces.
    private func put_break(depth: i64):
        if self.spaces <= 0:
            return
        self.out.append_ascii(10)
        for i in range(0, self.spaces * depth):
            self.out.append_ascii(32)

private func hex_digit(value: i64) -> i64:
    if value < 10:
        return i64(u32(digit_zero)) + value
    return 97 + value - 10
